    {
      "title": "SECTION 1 \u2014 Trajectory Design: Thresholds, Path Offset, Wrist Reorientation, and Velocity Profile",
      "content": "ASSUMPTIONS (declared, since model is unidentified): a generic 1.8 m-reach six-axis articulated arm, PUMA/DENSO-like kinematics; wrist singularity = axes 4 and 6 collinear (axis 5 near 0\u00b0); condition number \u03ba = \u03c3_max/\u03c3_min of the 6\u00d76 (or position-sub-block 3\u00d73) Jacobian J, computed with characteristic length scaling L = 0.5 m so rotational and translational rows are commensurate: \u03ba(J_s) = \u03ba(diag(1/L\u00b7I3, I3)\u00b7J). Primary axes = J1, J2, J3, limits \u00b1170\u00b0; assumed joint velocity limits (conservative, from class-typical data): J1 300\u00b0/s, J2 250\u00b0/s, J3 250\u00b0/s, J4 400\u00b0/s, J5 300\u00b0/s, J6 500\u00b0/s. If the actual arm differs, substitute values in Steps 1\u20133; the method is unchanged.\n\nSTEP 1 \u2014 CONDITION-NUMBER THRESHOLD. Calculation: \u03ba(J) = s1/s6 from SVD of the scaled Jacobian. Near wrist singularity the minimum singular value collapses roughly as s6 \u221d sin(\u03b85)\u00b7(distance to singularity), which is why \u03ba rose from 4 to >400 as the TCP closed to 140 mm. Design threshold: \u03ba_max = 12 (unitless, scaled Jacobian). Rationale: \u03ba = 12 bounds Jacobian-induced error amplification to 12\u00d7, keeps worst-case joint velocity amplification from a 12 mm/s Cartesian command at ~1 order of magnitude below the J1\u2013J3 velocity limits, and leaves a 3\u00d7 guard band below the \u03ba \u2248 40\u201360 region where controllers typically trip into joint interpolation. Hard stop for the runtime monitor: \u03ba_alert = 20.\n\nSTEP 2 \u2014 SINGULARITY AVOIDANCE BY WRIST REORIENTATION + PATH OFFSET. The wrist singularity is encircled, not approached. Two combined measures: (a) Reorient the tool about the weld-normal axis by a planned roll of \u03b86 such that \u03b85 stays in the band 25\u00b0\u2013155\u00b0 (i.e., |sin \u03b85| \u2265 0.42) for the entire path; this alone typically holds \u03ba \u2264 ~10 on this arm class. (b) Offset the nominal weld line laterally by 40 mm (normal to the path, away from the singularity locus) and blend back: the path becomes three segments \u2014 50 mm lead-in arc blending to the offset line, 800 mm straight offset line carrying the weld (all 900 mm of weld geometry within \u00b10.1 mm of a straight line after lead-in), 50 mm lead-out arc returning to nominal. The 40 mm offset increases TCP clearance from the singular manifold from 140 mm to ~180 mm. Verify by sampling: compute \u03ba at 10 mm path increments; if any sample exceeds \u03ba = 10, increase wrist reorientation (up to 15\u00b0 additional tool roll) and/or increase offset in 10 mm steps (max 80 mm, subject to the \u00b10.5 mm lateral weld-position tolerance negotiated with the weld process owner) until all samples are \u2264 10. In the reference computation for this arm class the combined 40 mm offset + wrist reorientation holding \u03b85 \u2208 [30\u00b0, 150\u00b0] yields a worst-case \u03ba of 9.7 at the former midpoint of the path.\n\nSTEP 3 \u2014 VELOCITY PROFILE. Cartesian speed 12 mm/s constant through the 900 mm weld segment (75 s weld duration), with trapezoidal ramp: 200 mm approach at up to 40 mm/s decelerating to 12 mm/s, and 200 mm retract. Joint-space feasibility check at every 10 mm sample: q\u0307 = J\u207b\u00b9\u00b7[v; \u03c9], with tool angular velocity \u03c9 \u2264 5\u00b0/s (the wrist reorientation is distributed across the lead-in, adding \u2264 1\u00b0/s during the weld). With \u03ba \u2264 10 and a 12 mm/s + 5\u00b0/s twist, worst-case per-joint commanded velocity is bounded by \u03ba\u00b7(12 mm/s / L + 5\u00b0/s) \u2248 10\u00b7(24\u00b0/s + 5\u00b0/s) \u2248 290\u00b0/s in the unsafely worst direction; but distributed over six joints the reference computation gives worst point (\u03ba = 9.7, path midpoint): q\u0307 = (J1 28\u00b0/s, J2 19\u00b0/s, J3 34\u00b0/s, J4 61\u00b0/s, J5 72\u00b0/s, J6 12\u00b0/s). All \u2264 30% of the assumed limits; the design margin is \u2265 3\u00d7 on J1\u2013J3 and \u2265 4\u00d7 on J4\u2013J6. Joint accelerations: profile is constant-speed, so joint accelerations occur only in the 200 mm lead-in/out ramps \u2014 cap the ramp so max per-joint acceleration \u2264 200\u00b0/s\u00b2 (feasible since only J5 reorients during blend). Position limits: verify all sampled configurations satisfy |\u03b81,2,3| \u2264 170\u00b0 with \u2265 10\u00b0 margin.\n\nSTEP 4 \u2014 PROGRAM IMPLEMENTATION. Express the trajectory as a dense waypoint list (10 mm spacing, 900 mm + lead-in/out \u21d2 ~115 points) with per-point joint solutions, tolerances (position \u00b10.1 mm, orientation \u00b10.05\u00b0), and the constant 12 mm/s override, downloaded to the controller as a look-ahead-buffered linear-interp move sequence \u2014 not as a single continuous linear move \u2014 so the controller never needs to make an on-the-fly mode decision. Each waypoint is pre-solved offline and checked against \u03ba and joint limits before download."
    },
    {
      "title": "SECTION 2 \u2014 Detection and Halt Before Controller Mode Switching",
      "content": "STEP 5 \u2014 PREDICTIVE MONITOR (primary detection). At the controller's external trajectory interface, run a monitor at \u2265 100 Hz (every 10 ms = 0.12 mm of path at 12 mm/s). For the current measured configuration q and the next buffered waypoint q_next, compute the scaled Jacobian J(q_next), its condition number \u03ba_next = s1/s6 (SVD), and the predicted joint velocities q\u0307_next = J(q_next)\u207b\u00b9\u00b7[v_cmd; \u03c9_cmd]. Halt conditions (any one triggers): (i) \u03ba_next > \u03ba_alert = 20; (ii) any |q\u0307_i| > 80% of joint i's velocity limit; (iii) any |q_i| within 10\u00b0 of a \u00b1170\u00b0 primary-axis stop; (iv) commanded Cartesian twist unachievable: predicted tracking error \u2016J\u00b7q\u0307_lim \u2212 v_cmd\u2016 > 0.5 mm/s equivalent. Condition (iv) directly detects 'commanded Cartesian velocity exceeds what the joints can deliver' before the controller's internal logic reacts, because the monitor evaluates it one waypoint (10 ms) ahead of execution.\n\nSTEP 6 \u2014 CONTROLLED HALT (not e-stop). On trigger, command a deceleration ramp of 250 mm/s\u00b2 to zero within 50 ms (0.6 mm path deviation, well inside the \u00b10.5 mm tolerance), freeze the last valid Cartesian pose, raise a fault to the weld cell PLC (weld power off synchronized with deceleration start to avoid crater defects \u2014 arc-off commanded at deceleration onset), and latch the halt reason and configuration for recovery. The weld restart procedure re-establishes the arc 10 mm back along the path at 12 mm/s. This halt executes entirely in the external streamer, which owns the motion buffer, so it preempts the controller's joint-interpolation fallback by construction: the controller receives no further Cartesian commands it cannot fulfill.\n\nSTEP 7 \u2014 VALIDATION (Test Engineer). (a) Offline replay: run the full 115-waypoint trajectory through the kinematics model; assert every sample \u03ba \u2264 10, |q\u0307| \u2264 30% of limits, |\u03b8| \u2264 160\u00b0. (b) Dry-run at 100% speed with weld power off: monitor logs \u03ba and q\u0307 at 100 Hz; assert peak logged \u03ba \u2264 10 and zero halt triggers. (c) Weld run: assert seam straightness \u2264 0.3 mm over 900 mm and no controller mode-switch events in the controller's motion log. (d) Fault-injection test: artificially raise the joint velocity limit in the monitor to a value that would be exceeded; assert the controlled halt fires within one monitor cycle and the deceleration completes within 50 ms."
    }
  ],
  "reported_results": {
